pandemic time when people were missing out on the weddings they'd imagined or not being able to have baby showers for very longed-for babies or maybe even having to delay having a child at all, not being able to be with people that they would want to spend time and connect with. And the idea that it could be not for someone that had died, you know, really felt like it was an important kind of cultural realisation, really. And so, yeah, you absolutely are able and allowed to, to be empowered, to grieve for something that you realise you've lost out on or haven't experienced that you would have wanted to. And, you know, sometimes when I'm working with clients who've been through challenging upbringings, sometimes we just think about what would have been the minimum thing that actually would have helped make your life better.
疫情期间，当时人们错过了他们想象中的婚礼，或者无法为期待已久的宝宝举办迎婴派对，甚至不得不推迟生育计划，无法与他们想要共度时光和建立联系的人在一起。这种悲伤不一定是因为某人去世了，这在文化认知上确实是一个重要的转折点。所以，是的，你完全有能力、也有权利去为那些你意识到自己错失或未能体验到但又渴望的东西感到悲伤。你知道，有时当我与那些成长经历充满挑战的客户合作时，我们有时会思考，到底什么是能让你的生活变得更好的最基本的东西。

